{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789218131,"nonce":"XfB7ugVNP5AgOea7IcjPAwJ1c/c=","title":"A for over an unrecognised iterator compiled to an infinite loop","message":"typed_cfg's For arm recognised a counted loop only as a Call to a\nvariable named `range` with two or three arguments. Anything else,\nincluding the TypedAST's own Range node and `range(end)` with one\nargument, fell to a fallback marked TODO that emitted a loop with no\nexit condition. Exit 0, wrong program, nothing said. Same class as the\nwrong-arity call that used to skip a function.\n\nReproducer: a Python `for i in range(10)` lowered to a Range node; the\ntest hung in JIT code, HIR showed the loop variable undef and an\nunconditional back edge.\n\nFix: recognise range(end), range(start, end), range(start, end, step),\nstart..end and start..=end; refuse everything else with a lowering\nerror naming the iterator.","files":null}]}